    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-183869",
      "problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n${[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{n} & {2}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$",
